---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { host } from '../config';
---

<Layout title="Privacy" description={`What happens to the details you give when you book time with ${host.name}.`}>
  <article class="doc pb-16 pt-6 sm:pt-10">
    <h1 class="doc-h1">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[0.875rem] text-muted">Updated October 23, 2026</p>

    <div class="privacy mt-8">
      <h2 class="doc-h2">What you give</h2>
      <p>
        When you book, you give your name, your email, and, if you like, a note. The page also sends the time, your
        time zone, and the topic if you picked one.
      </p>
      <p>
        To show times that suit you, the page uses your device’s time zone, and Cloudflare’s rough guess at your time
        zone from your IP address in case your device is set to another one. The only one saved is the time zone you book
        in, which goes into the calendar event. The page never works out where you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        If none of the times work, you can send a note instead: your name, your email, and what you write, along with
        your time zone and the topic if you picked one.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2">Where it goes</h2>
      <p>
        A booking goes into one event on {host.firstName}’s Google Calendar, with you as the guest. Google sends you the
        invite and the Meet link. Spoiled NYC advertising bookings use {host.firstName}’s work calendar. A note goes
        to {host.firstName}’s Gmail inbox as one email, sent from his own address
        with yours to reply to. There is no separate database, no mailing list, no analytics, and no tracking cookies.
        Schedulum itself stores one thing in your browser: whether you chose the light or dark theme.
      </p>
      <p>{host.firstName} may also get a one-line alert with your name, the time, and the topic if you picked one.</p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2">Who else sees your visit</h2>
      <p>
        The site runs on Cloudflare. Cloudflare keeps request logs for a few days so {host.firstName} can fix problems,
        and those logs include the addresses of the pages you open, such as your reschedule link.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you book or send a note, Cloudflare Turnstile checks that a person, not a bot, is doing it. It reads signals such as your IP
        address and browser, as its
        <a class="link" href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">privacy addendum</a> describes.
      </p>
      <p>The fonts load from Google Fonts, so Google sees your IP address and browser when a page loads.</p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2">What it does with {host.firstName}’s Google account</h2>
      <p>
        Schedulum reads when {host.firstName} is free or busy, so it only offers open times. It creates, moves, and
        cancels the events it booked. It doesn’t read the details of anything else on the calendar.
      </p>
      <p>
        It can send email from {host.firstName}’s Gmail, and uses that only to deliver visitors’ notes to him. It can’t
        read his mail.
      </p>
      <p>
        Schedulum’s use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
        <a class="link" href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
          >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
        >, including the Limited Use requirements. Nothing is sold, shared for advertising, or used to train AI models.
      </p>

      <h2 class="doc-h2">Changing your mind</h2>
      <p>
        Your invite has a link to reschedule or cancel. Cancelling deletes the event. To have anything else removed,
        email <a class="link" href={`mailto:${host.email}`}>{host.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10"><a class="btn-quiet" href="/">Book a conversation</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .privacy :global(h2) {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
  }
  .privacy :global(p) {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
</style>
